-  "body": "This is for reading a piece from a field you do not know yet. Nothing here reads it for you: each\nstep sends you back into the author’s own words better prepared. If Spideryarn itself is new to\nyou, start with [Your first article](/help/first-article).\n\n## Say what you know\n\n- **Fill in About you** on your [reader profile](/help/reader-profile): your background, what you\n  know well, what you are rusty on. It changes what gets explained and how much, never what the\n  article says.\n- **Answer “Why are you reading this?”** for the article. The quiz, for one, aims more of its\n  questions at what you said.\n\n## Before you read\n\n- **Open [Ideas](/help/mode-ideas)** and read **What you need to bring**: the ideas the piece leans\n  on and never states. One that is new to you explains why a passage felt opaque.\n- **Open the [Glossary](/help/mode-glossary)** early. It defines terms from how this piece uses\n  them, and afterwards they stay underlined in every mode.\n- **See how the piece is built** with [Structure](/help/mode-structure) and [the\n  spine](/help/spine): the same parts, drawn two ways.\n- **Walk a [Skim](/help/mode-skim) route** for the shape of a paper. Every stop is the author’s\n  sentence, in place, with a cue for what to look for.\n\n## While you read\n\n- **Read in [Plain](/help/mode-plain)**, and point at an underlined term when you need its\n  definition.\n- **Press the ? in [the margin](/help/gutter)** beside a paragraph you cannot follow. The answer\n  opens as a [chat](/help/mode-chat) you can carry on.\n- **Highlight and write notes** as you go — see [Comments and bookmarks](/help/comments).\n  **Comments** in the bottom bar lists everything you have marked, in article order.\n- **Try [FAQ](/help/mode-faq)** on a dense piece. It writes no answers: each question points to the\n  passages that respond to it. It is one of the [experimental\n  features](/help/experimental-features).\n\n## Afterwards\n\n- **Open [Learn](/help/mode-learn)** when you want it to stick. In **Recall** you say what you took\n  from the piece and find out where you went wrong; **Tutorial** takes a little of the piece at a\n  time; **Quiz** asks up to twenty short questions.\n- **Go back to Ideas** for **What this piece adds**, a short list of the ideas worth taking away.\n- **Read the [Summary](/help/mode-summary)** to check you came away with the main points.\n\nWhen something matters, follow the link and read the passage: see [Is the AI reading the article\nfor me?](/help/questions#faq-is-the-ai-reading-for-me). For every other mode, see [Which mode\nwhen](/help/modes)."
+  "body": "This is for reading a piece from a field you do not know yet. Nothing here reads it for you: each\nstep sends you back into the author’s own words better prepared. If Spideryarn itself is new to\nyou, start with [Your first article](/help/first-article).\n\n## Say what you know\n\n- **Fill in About you** on your [reader profile](/help/reader-profile): your background, what you\n  know well, what you are rusty on. It changes what gets explained and how much, never what the\n  article says.\n- **Answer “Why are you reading this?”** for the article. The quiz, for one, aims more of its\n  questions at what you said.\n\n## Before you read\n\n- **Open [Ideas](/help/mode-ideas)** and read **What you need to bring**: the ideas the piece leans\n  on and never states. One that is new to you explains why a passage felt opaque.\n- **Open the [Glossary](/help/mode-glossary)** early. It defines terms from how this piece uses\n  them, and afterwards they stay underlined in every mode.\n- **See how the piece is built** with [Structure](/help/mode-structure) and [the\n  spine](/help/spine): the same parts, drawn two ways.\n- **Walk a [Skim](/help/mode-skim) route** for the shape of a paper. Every stop is the author’s\n  sentence, in place, with a question to read it with where one helps.\n\n## While you read\n\n- **Read in [Plain](/help/mode-plain)**, and point at an underlined term when you need its\n  definition.\n- **Press the ? in [the margin](/help/gutter)** beside a paragraph you cannot follow. The answer\n  opens as a [chat](/help/mode-chat) you can carry on.\n- **Highlight and write notes** as you go — see [Comments and bookmarks](/help/comments).\n  **Comments** in the bottom bar lists everything you have marked, in article order.\n- **Try [FAQ](/help/mode-faq)** on a dense piece. It writes no answers: each question points to the\n  passages that respond to it. It is one of the [experimental\n  features](/help/experimental-features).\n\n## Afterwards\n\n- **Open [Learn](/help/mode-learn)** when you want it to stick. In **Recall** you say what you took\n  from the piece and find out where you went wrong; **Tutorial** takes a little of the piece at a\n  time; **Quiz** asks up to twenty short questions.\n- **Go back to Ideas** for **What this piece adds**, a short list of the ideas worth taking away.\n- **Read the [Summary](/help/mode-summary)** to check you came away with the main points.\n\nWhen something matters, follow the link and read the passage: see [Is the AI reading the article\nfor me?](/help/questions#faq-is-the-ai-reading-for-me). For every other mode, see [Which mode\nwhen](/help/modes)."
  },
  {
   "anchor": "for-reviewers",
@@ -317,7 +317,7 @@
   "summary": "A route through the piece's quotes, a little deeper each time round.",
   "keywords": "skimming quick read route spiral trajectory stops depth gist more most tour fast overview spiral route trajectory skim read speed read quick read preview scan skim",
   "experimental": false,
-  "body": "## In short\n\nSkim picks out a handful of passages which, read on their own, give you a good sense of what the\npiece is about. Think of it as an alternative to a summary that keeps you in the article: every stop\nis a passage from the article, in place, with a short cue saying what to look for. Along the way it\nshows what Glossary and Ideas have already found at each stop, such as an unfamiliar term explained.\nGo round quickly with a few stops, then again in more detail, then again in more.\n\n(Picture: Pressing the right arrow twice: the outlined passage in the article moves to the next stop each time, with Next stop and the next cue beneath it. Caption: Each press of → or Next stop moves to the next stop, and the article scrolls to it.)\n\n## How it works\n\nA route through the piece's quotes, a little deeper each time round.\n\nA short model call over the article's Quotes and its key Ideas — never the rest of its prose — puts the Quotes in an order and gives each a depth, so each pass covers as many of the Ideas as the quotes reach, shaped by your profile if you have one. When there are no Quotes or Ideas yet, they are made first; finding the Ideas is the longer part.\n\n## When to use it\n\nWhen you need the shape of a paper fast but do not want a summary in place of the text: every stop\nis a passage from the article, in place, in context. **Gist** is a handful of stops, **More** about a\ndozen, **Most** a larger share of the piece. The order is planned for you — the results first, say,\nthen a quick look at the methods — not the article’s. Only whoever added the article can plan a\nroute; visitors to a shared article can walk one already planned.\n\n## Reading it\n\n(Picture: A Skim stop: the route’s stops on the left with the current one open, and its passage highlighted in the article on the right, with Next stop below. Caption: A stop on the route: the cue says what to look for, the passage is marked in place, and Next stop moves on.)\n\n- <kbd>‹</kbd> <kbd>›</kbd> at the top, or <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> while reading, step from stop\n  to stop. **Next stop ›**, in the text under the current stop, does the same, and at the end of a\n  pass becomes **More detail ›**.\n- Each stop has a short cue above it: what to *look for* in the passage, never what it found.\n- A deeper pass has its own new stops and may keep earlier ones where the new ones lean on them.\n  When a route has any, small dots under each stop’s number show which passes it is in, shallowest\n  first: more than one filled, and you may have read it already.\n- Under the current stop, a card gathers what other modes have already found there: **Terms it\n  uses** and **Ideas it bears on**.\n- The dot beside each row shows how far through the article that stop is, so you can see the route\n  jump about.\n- **Reading for:** at the top is what you said you want from this article; the route is planned\n  around it. **Edit** changes it."
